Contribution:
I worked on the UML class diagrams for Stages 2 and 3 of Restaurant Rush. I identified the classes,
their attributes and methods, and the relationships between them.

1. I learned how to apply inheritance, abstraction, and polymorphism when designing UML class diagrams.
More importantly, I gained a better understanding of how to organise class relationships to make a 
system more reusable and maintainable.

2. In Stage 2, I represented how VIPCustomer and CriticCustomer inherit from Customer while having 
different behaviours. In Stage 3, I represented the new staff hierarchy, where Waiter and Host inherit 
from SeatingStaff, while SeatingStaff and Chef inherit from ControllableStaff.

3. The hardest part was identifying the correct relationships and multiplicities between classes,
especially when the same class was referenced in different ways. For example, GameController stores a 
Waiter object as an attribute, whereas SeatingStaff only uses Customer and Table as method parameters,
so I had to determine whether to use a solid association or dashed dependency.

4. I went through the Java files individually and checked how each class interacted with the others.
I used attributes to identify associations, method parameters and local variables to identify 
dependencies, and extends to identify inheritance. I also checked the multiplicities and arrow 
directions against the code to make sure the diagrams represented the actual class relationships.
